{
  "suite": "kdf_pace",
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "description": "PACE key derivation: ICAO 9303 P11 App G K_pi chain (AES-128 only; G.1/G.2 both use AES-128) plus G.1/G.2 session-key derivations from the published shared secrets. Synthetic cases: DESede PACE_MODE, nonce-present (CA-v2-only, no ICAO source), AES-192/256 SHA-256 path, \"AES-nnn\" alias forms, error paths. The pace.createPaceKey op covers the password codec f(pi) (P11 §9.7.3 Table 14 for MRZ/CAN, TR-03110-3 Table 5 + D.2.1.4 for PIN/PUK) together with the K_pi it derives. The two MRZ rows are published (App I.1 and App G); every CAN/PIN/CUK row is synthetic, generated by conformance/tools/pace_cam_password_oracle.py, because no ICAO worked example uses a non-MRZ password. The charset rows pin the charset guard: CAN/PIN/CUK reject the ISO 8859-1 unassigned ranges (0x00-0x1F, 0x7F-0x9F) and anything above 0xFF, while MRZ, hashed rather than encoded, stays unguarded. The inert-paceKeyReference case is untouched: KDF_pi(pi) = KDF(f(pi), 3) is password-type-independent, so no derivation branches on the reference byte; only f(pi) and the DO83 byte differ by type. createPaceKey rows are dart-only: upstream has createPaceKey but only ever feeds it an MRZ, so a swift op is S3-gated.",
  "runners": [
    "dart",
    "swift"
  ],
  "cases": [
